      "content": "#### Division of tasks between the two species\n\nWe assign environmental sensing and naringenin synthesis to *S. epidermidis*, and signal reception and functional protein expression to *E. coli*. This division draws on the native HssRS heme-response system in *S. epidermidis* and previous applications of FdeR-based naringenin-sensing circuits in *E. coli*.[12,13,16]\n\nWe selected EcN as the final receiver chassis based on its established probiotic use. BL21 is used for initial response testing, and the two receiver hosts are characterized separately.[15] Sensing, synthesis, and reception can also be tested independently before evaluating communication through naringenin.\n\n#### Naringenin as the communication signal\n\nWe compared four candidate signals: 2,4-diacetylphloroglucinol (DAPG), salicylate, indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), and naringenin. Selection considered receiver response strength, potential background signals from wound microorgan",
