      "source": "Design (EN)",
      "title": "Proof-of-concept experiments",
      "content": "We assessed sender heme sensitivity using disc diffusion and growth curves, and characterized HmSr and NrSr independently. Independent characterization evaluates the sensing, synthesis, and reception functions separately. Subsequent supernatant-transfer experiments will test whether sender products induce a receiver response.\n\n#### Heme sensitivity and growth inhibition\n\nHemin exposure has been reported to inhibit *S. epidermidis* growth under the tested conditions.[16] We therefore compared heme sensitivity in *S. epidermidis* and *Staphylococcus aureus* using disc diffusion and liquid-culture growth curves before characterizing HmSr responses.\n\nDisc diffusion assays used DMSO as a solvent control to assess local growth inhibition. Liquid-culture OD₆₀₀ time courses assessed growth across heme conditions relative to the untreated group. These complementary assays provided the growth context for interpreting HmSr induction and supported",
